# The Modern `@use` Rule 📦

The `@use` rule loads mixins, functions, and variables from another Sass file as a **namespaced module**.

```scss_not
// _colors.scss
$primary: #4f46e5;
$secondary: #64748b;

@mixin rounded-box {
  border-radius: 8px;
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

```scss_not
// _button.scss
@use 'colors'; // Loads _colors.scss into the 'colors' namespace!

.btn-primary {
  background-color: colors.$primary; // Clean namespace access!
  @include colors.rounded-box;
}
```

> ⚡ **Key Benefit:** It is 100% obvious that `$primary` came from the `colors` module!

# Namespaces and Aliasing with `as` 🏷️

By default, the namespace matches the file name without extension or underscore. You can customize this namespace using `as`.

## 1. Custom Alias Namespace

```scss_not
@use 'theme-colors' as theme;

.badge {
  background: theme.$accent;
}
```

## 2. Unprefixed Namespace (`as *`)

If you load a module with `as *`, all its members become available without any namespace prefix (similar to legacy behavior):

```scss_not
@use 'theme-colors' as *;

.badge {
  background: $accent; // Direct access without namespace prefix!
}
```

> ⚠️ **Caution:** Use `as *` sparingly, as it reintroduces potential name collisions.

# Private Members in Sass Modules 🔒

With the module system, you can define truly private variables, mixins, or functions that cannot be accessed outside their defining file!

## How to Make a Member Private

Prefix the member name with a hyphen `-` or underscore `_`:

```scss_not
// _math-utils.scss
$-golden-ratio: 1.618; // PRIVATE variable (starts with -)
$base-unit: 8px;       // PUBLIC variable

@function -compute-scale($step) { // PRIVATE function
  @return $step * $-golden-ratio * $base-unit;
}

@function get-size($step) { // PUBLIC function
  @return -compute-scale($step);
}
```

If another file tries to access `math-utils.$-golden-ratio`, the compiler throws an error!

# Configuring Modules with `with` 🎛️

When using `@use`, you can configure variables defined with `!default` directly at import time using the `with` clause.

```scss_not
// _library.scss
$button-bg: #3b82f6 !default;
$button-radius: 4px !default;

.btn {
  background: $button-bg;
  border-radius: $button-radius;
}
```

```scss_not
// main.scss
@use 'library' with (
  $button-bg: #10b981,
  $button-radius: 9999px
);
```

> ⚡ **Advantage:** No more global variable mutation hacks. Configuration is explicit, clean, and locally scoped!

# Forwarding Modules with `@forward` ⏩

The `@forward` rule loads a Sass stylesheet and makes its mixins, functions, and variables available when your stylesheet is loaded via `@use`.

## Creating a Barrel File (`_index.scss`)

Instead of requiring consumers to `@use` 10 separate partials, create an `_index.scss` that forwards them all:

```scss_not
// scss/components/_index.scss
@forward 'buttons';
@forward 'cards';
@forward 'alerts';
@forward 'navbar';
```

```scss_not
// main.scss
@use 'components'; // Automatically loads scss/components/_index.scss!

.my-card {
  @include components.card-style;
}
```

# Prefixing & Visibility in `@forward` 🏷️

When forwarding, you can add prefixes to member names to avoid collisions or selectively hide private items.

## 1. Adding Prefixes (`as prefix-*`)

```scss_not
// _theme.scss
@forward 'colors' as color-*;
@forward 'fonts' as font-*;
```

Consumers now access `theme.$color-primary` and `theme.$font-heading`.

## 2. Hiding or Showing Members

```scss_not
// Only forward specific items:
@forward 'mixins' show flex-center, truncate-text;

// Forward everything EXCEPT internal items:
@forward 'helpers' hide internal-calculate, debug-border;
```

# Built-In Module: `sass:math` 🔢

Native slash division (`/`) was deprecated because CSS uses `/` as a separator (e.g. `font: 16px/1.5`). Modern Sass performs math using `sass:math`.

```scss_not
@use 'sass:math';

.container {
  // Safe division without ambiguity
  width: math.div(100%, 3); // 33.3333333333%

  // Rounding
  margin: math.round(15.6px); // 16px
  padding: math.floor(15.9px); // 15px

  // Extremes & Powers
  max-width: math.max(300px, 500px, 400px); // 500px
  z-index: math.pow(2, 4); // 16

  // Geometry
  line-height: math.sqrt(4); // 2
}
```

> ⚡ **Always Use `math.div()`:** Instead of `$width / 2`, write `math.div($width, 2)`.

# Built-In Module: `sass:color` 🎨

Inspect and manipulate colors dynamically using `sass:color`.

```scss_not
@use 'sass:color';

$brand: #4f46e5;

.button {
  background-color: $brand;

  &:hover {
    // Lighten color by adjusting lightness
    background-color: color.adjust($brand, $lightness: 10%);
  }

  &:active {
    // Darken color by adjusting lightness
    background-color: color.adjust($brand, $lightness: -10%);
  }

  // Adjust transparency / alpha channel
  box-shadow: 0 4px 12px color.change($brand, $alpha: 0.35);

  // Color blending
  border-color: color.mix($brand, #ffffff, 60%);
}
```

# Built-In Modules: `sass:map` & `sass:list` 🗺️

Manipulate data collections programmatically.

```scss_not
@use 'sass:map';
@use 'sass:list';

$grid-breakpoints: (
  'sm': 576px,
  'md': 768px,
  'lg': 992px
);

// Map operations
$has-md: map.has-key($grid-breakpoints, 'md'); // true
$md-width: map.get($grid-breakpoints, 'md');   // 768px
$merged: map.merge($grid-breakpoints, ('xl': 1200px));

// List operations
$pad-scale: (4px, 8px, 16px, 24px);
$first-pad: list.nth($pad-scale, 1); // 4px
$appended: list.append($pad-scale, 32px);
```

# Built-In Module: `sass:string` 🔤

The `sass:string` module provides utilities for inspecting, slicing, and modifying strings.

```scss_not
@use 'sass:string';

$quote: 'Hello World';
$len: string.length($quote); // 11
$slice: string.slice($quote, 1, 5); // 'Hello'
$upper: string.to-upper-case($quote); // 'HELLO WORLD'
$lower: string.to-lower-case($quote); // 'hello world'

// Unquote string for raw CSS keywords
$font-raw: string.unquote('Inter, sans-serif');
body {
  font-family: $font-raw;
}
```

# Production main.scss Master Manifest 📜

Here is how a clean `main.scss` appears in a professional enterprise app:

```scss_not
// 1. Abstract utilities
@use 'abstracts/variables' as vars;
@use 'abstracts/mixins' as mix;
@use 'abstracts/functions' as func;

// 2. Base resets
@use 'base/reset';
@use 'base/typography';

// 3. Layout structural blocks
@use 'layout/navbar';
@use 'layout/grid';
@use 'layout/footer';

// 4. UI Components
@use 'components/buttons';
@use 'components/cards';
@use 'components/modals';

// 5. Vendor integrations
@use 'vendors/prism-theme';
```

# Common Module System Pitfalls ⚠️

## 1. Multiple `@use` Declarations Must Come First
All `@use` and `@forward` rules **must** appear at the very top of your stylesheet before any CSS rules:

```scss_not
// WRONG: Rule before @use!
body { margin: 0; }
@use 'colors'; // ERROR!
```

## 2. Circular Dependencies
File A cannot `@use 'file-b'` if File B also `@use 'file-a'`. Keep dependencies flowing in a single direction (e.g. components depend on abstracts, never vice-versa).
